.TH init "" "Administration"
.SH NAME
init \- process control initialization
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fB/etc/init [\fIswapper \fB[\fIfile ...\fB]]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
init -- process control initialization
Usage:	/etc/init [ swapper [ file ... ] ]
If 'swapper' is given, it is loaded and started as a program.
Each further 'file' argument is brought up in order: a name ending
in '.sl' is started as a shared library, anything else is a driver
loaded with '/etc/load'.  Empty arguments are skipped.
.HE
The kernel starts
.B init
as the first process, with process identifier 1, and
.B init
runs for as long as the system is up.
Its job is to bring the system from boot into multi-user mode and
then to keep the login process running on every enabled terminal.
.PP
If given an argument,
.B init
loads it as a
.I swapper
program with
.BR fork ()
and
.BR execve ().
Every further argument names a
.I file
to bring up, in order.
A name ending in
.B .sl
is a shared library:
.B init
starts it with
.BR fork ()
and
.BR execve (),
and the child becomes the library's resident holder.
Any other name is a
.I driver
file, which
.B init
loads by running
.BR /etc/load .
An empty argument, or a shared library whose file does not exist, is
silently skipped; the system runs without it.
.PP
The argument list itself is composed by the kernel:
it always passes the console driver and
.BR /lib/libc.sl ,
and adds
.B /lib/libhrgfx.sl
only when its boot-time video probe found the hi-res display card.
.B init
applies no policy of its own.
.SH "Boot Sequence"
.B init
first tries to create the file
.BR /etc/boottime ;
this doubles as a check that the root file system is writable.
If root came up read-only, because it was not cleanly unmounted, the
.BR creat ()
fails and
.B init
repairs the root file system automatically:
it finds the device root is mounted on by matching the device numbers
of the entries in
.B /dev
against that of
.BR / ,
runs
.B "check \-s"
on that device (the file system is quiescent, since nothing can be
open for writing on a read-only root), and then promotes root to
read/write with
.BR "mount \-w" ,
which re-reads the repaired super block and refuses if the file
system is still dirty.
.PP
.B init
records the boot in
.BR /usr/adm/wtmp ,
and installs handlers for
.B SIGHUP
and
.BR SIGQUIT .
With root writable it goes straight to multi-user:
it runs
.B /etc/rc
if it exists, to complete multi-user setup, then reads
.B /etc/ttys
and starts
.B getty
on every enabled line listed there.
There is no single-user shell on a normal boot.
.PP
The interactive single-user shell survives only as an emergency:
if the automatic repair fails (root is left read-only), if
.B SIGHUP
asks for single-user mode, or if every terminal in
.B /etc/ttys
is disabled,
.B init
spawns a shell on
.BR /dev/console ,
with prompt
.B "# "
(or
.B "(ro)# "
while root is still read-only).
When that shell exits (typically because the operator typed an
end-of-file), and root is writable by then,
.B init
proceeds to multi-user as above; if root is still read-only it
prints a warning, naming
.B "check \-s"
and
.BR "mount \-w" ,
and starts the shell again.
.PP
.B init
then waits for its children to exit.
If a
.B getty
(or the process it eventually replaced itself with, by way of
.BR login )
on a given line exits,
.B init
clears that line's
.B utmp
entry, restores the terminal's mode and ownership, and restarts
.B getty
on it, unless the line has since been disabled in
.BR /etc/ttys .
Other terminating children are simply reaped.
.SH Signals
.B init
responds to two signals:
.IP \fBSIGQUIT\fR 0.9i
Re-reads
.BR /etc/ttys ,
starting
.B getty
on any newly enabled line and killing the
.B getty
(or login session) on any line that has been disabled.
Sent to
.B init
with
.BR "kill \-quit 1" .
.IP \fBSIGHUP\fR
Sends
.B SIGKILL
to every process and returns the system to single-user mode.
Sent to
.B init
with
.BR "kill \-1 1" .
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 16n
\fB/dev/console\fR	console terminal
\fB/etc/boottime\fR	boot-time stamp, and root-writable probe
\fB/etc/rc\fR	multi-user startup command file
\fB/etc/ttys\fR	terminals to enable
\fB/etc/utmp\fR	currently logged-in lines
\fB/etc/load\fR	driver loader, run for each \fIdriver\fR argument
\fB/lib/libc.sl\fR	shared C library, started at boot
\fB/lib/libhrgfx.sl\fR	shared graphics library (hi-res machines)
\fB/usr/adm/wtmp\fR	login accounting log
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR check ,
.BR getty ,
.BR kill ,
.BR login ,
.BR mount ,
.B sh
.SH NOTES
The writability of the root file system is probed directly by
attempting to create
.BR /etc/boottime ,
and a dirty root is repaired and remounted in place; the system is
never rebooted for a repair.
While root remains read-only \- automatic repair failed \-
.B init
refuses to run
.B /etc/rc
and stays in the single-user emergency shell.
